Z||000002||fropuff||04-13-2001||11:09 AM||||I'm sorry about that.  I didn't see that you wanted to polish the wheels, not strip them.  I found that Mothers makes the best mag wheel polish.  Comes in a little red can with a white cover.  I'll still leave the stripping instructions if anyone else wants them; sorry about that.  <P>-Well, I did my GT wheels last summer and they turned out very good!  I just did the outer aluminum lip, as the clear coat was peeling and yellowed.  Anyway, I went to the paint shop and bought two cans of plastic stripper.  It's called Bulldog Plastic Parts Srtipper, or something like that.  It is Bulldog; in a yellow can with a bulldog on it.  Anyway, I cut out a cardboard template with a circle in the center to cover the gray of the wheels, as I didn't want to strip that part.  I didn't tape around the tires, but that's because I was getting new ones anyway.  It's a good idea to mask them off, the stripper eats away at the rubber.  Then I started spraying.  I did one wheel at a time.  Spray a good amount of stripper all the way around the outer lip, then wait around ten to fifteen minutes.  When you can see the clear coat is lifted, either wipe it off with a rag or pressure wash it off.  The first coat usually doesn't take it off.  There will still be some in the small grooves.  Spray a little more on, this time spraying a larger amount in the areas that didn't come off the first time.  Let this sit around 20 minutes.  Do the same thing; wipe off or pressure wash.  This should take care of most of the clear coat.  There still might be some left in the small grooves, but just go back to that area and spray some more, let it sit, and wipe or spray off.  I didn't sand my wheels when I was done, as I like the look of the mill marks on the outer lip.  I just bought some Mothers mag wheel polish and polished the outer lip.  You can see black crap coming out of the aluminum; this is a good thing.  The wheels will look great when done with them.  I did not clear coat over mine, and don't plan to, but if you do, either buy a spray can of clear coat or bring it to a paint guy and have him do it for you.  This about covers it.  By the way, you might want to wear gloves and safety goggles.  Got some in the corner of my eye where the tear gland is; don't ask what I said.  It also burns your skin.  Be sure not to kneel on some stripper that's sitting on the ground; spray it off each time.  Good lucK!<P>Welcome to the forum!<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||10
